course: dictionary
lesson: 7000
title: Seeing from two places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hold a pencil at arm's length and close your left eye, then close your right eye. The pencil seems to jump against the wall behind it. This shift is called parallax: an object looks like it moves against a far background when you view it from two different places.
explain: «Astronomers measure distances to nearby stars by parallax.» A star seems to shift slightly against far-off stars as Earth moves round the Sun. The farther apart the two places you look from, the bigger the shift.
explain: In science and maths you must read a ruler or measuring jug with your eye straight in front of the mark. Looking from the side gives a wrong reading, called «parallax error».
fact: Parallax is the apparent shift of an object against a background when you view it from two places.
fact: Parallax is an uncountable noun, so say «some parallax», not «a parallax».
fact: The farther apart the two viewing places are, the bigger the shift you see.
fact: In 1838 Friedrich Bessel measured the parallax of the star 61 Cygni, the first star distance ever measured.
exercise: «Astronomers measure distances to nearby stars by parallax.» What is parallax mainly used for here? | Naming the planets | Making stars brighter | Turning telescopes round | *Measuring how far away stars are
exercise: Why does a pencil seem to jump when you close each eye in turn? | The pencil is made of glass | Your eyes need glasses | The pencil is moving very fast | *You are seeing it from two different places
exercise: Why must you read a measuring jug with your eye straight in front? | *To avoid «parallax error» | So the liquid gets warmer | To make the jug look bigger | To stop the jug from spilling
test: The farther apart the two places you look from, what happens to the shift? | Disappears | Stays the same | Gets smaller | *Gets bigger
test: In 1838 Friedrich Bessel measured the parallax of the star 61 Cygni. What was special about this measurement? | It was the brightest star ever seen | It was the first planet ever found | It was the first photo of the Sun | *It was the first star distance ever measured
exam: Ben closes one eye, then the other, and a nearby pencil seems to jump against the wall. What is this effect called? | Reflection | Gravity | *Parallax | Refraction
